<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Yamhad</h1><p class="st-meta">1810 BCE – 1517 BCE (approx.) · capital Halab</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yamhad, the Amorite great kingdom of Aleppo, which dominated northern Syria in the 18th and 17th centuries BCE until the Hittites destroyed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumu-epuh">Sumu-epuh</a> founded the kingdom at Halab, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, around 1810 BCE. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim I</a> made Yamhad the leading power of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> letter claims twenty kings followed him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its great kings ruled through dependent kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mukish">Alalakh</a> in the Kingdom of Mukish and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ebla">Ebla</a>, and their prestige rested on the temple of the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> citadel. Wealth came from the trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Hurrian culture spread widely among the elite.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> attacked Yamhad, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> c. 1595 BCE. A reduced kingdom survived until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire</a> absorbed it in the 16th century BCE. The Aleppo storm-god temple remained venerated for centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yamhad.jpg" alt="Yamhad" width="320" height="254"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Attar-Aram syria · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamhad_and_Vassals.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1810 BCE – 1517 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Halab</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sumu-epuh, Yarim-Lim I, Hammurabi I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amorite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Yamhad, Halab</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1664&amp;focus=yamhad" aria-label="Yamhad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5615 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yamhad lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5935 1127L5929 1141M5935 1127L5852 1002M5935 1127L5996 1079M5935 1127L5916 1126"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5929 1141h0M5852 1002h0M5996 1079h0M5916 1126h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5935 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5935 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1664&amp;focus=yamhad">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

